// Formative practice quiz for remotes-clone-push-fetch-pull. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design.
$CATEGORY: git-linux-ai-foundations/git-foundations/remotes-clone-push-fetch-pull

::remotes-clone-push-fetch-pull o1 - which key GitHub gets::Cloning over SSH fails with Permission denied (publickey). A forum post tells you to paste the contents of \~/.ssh/id_ed25519 into GitHub's SSH keys settings. What should you do?{
=Paste the contents of \~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub instead#Correct. The .pub file is the public key, which is safe to share. GitHub uses it to recognise the matching private key on your computer.
~Follow the post and paste \~/.ssh/id_ed25519#Not quite. id_ed25519 is the private key. It never leaves your computer; anyone who has it can act as you.
~Paste both files so GitHub has the full pair#Not quite. GitHub needs only the public key. Sharing the private key gives away the secret half.
~Email \~/.ssh/id_ed25519 to the repository owner so they can add it#Not quite. The private key is never sent to anyone. You add your own public key to your GitHub account.
}

::remotes-clone-push-fetch-pull o2 - read main against origin/main::After git fetch, your log shows main at C8 and origin/main at C9, a commit your teammate pushed. What does this tell you?{
=GitHub's main was at C9 when you fetched; your own main and files are still at C8#Correct. origin/main is your computer's note of where GitHub's main was. Fetch moved only that note; your main has not moved.
~Your main already contains C9, because fetch updates your branch#Not quite. Fetch downloads commits and moves only origin/main. Updating your own main needs git pull.
~GitHub's main is behind yours and needs a push#Not quite. origin/main is ahead, since it has C9, which your main does not have yet.
~origin/main is a branch on GitHub that you are now editing directly#Not quite. origin/main lives in your clone. It is a remote-tracking ref recording where GitHub's main was.
}

::remotes-clone-push-fetch-pull o3 - what pull changes::You have no new local commits. A teammate pushed C8 to GitHub's main. You run git pull on main. What changes on your computer?{
=origin/main and your main both move to C8, and your files update to match#Correct. git pull fetches, which moves origin/main, then moves your branch forward to match. Nothing on GitHub changes.
~Only origin/main moves; your main and files stay at the old commit#Not quite. That is what git fetch alone does. git pull also moves your branch forward.
~Nothing, because pull stops whenever GitHub has new commits#Not quite. Pull stops only when both sides gained new commits. Here only GitHub did, so your branch simply moves forward.
~GitHub's main moves back to match your main#Not quite. Pull never changes GitHub. Only git push uploads to the remote.
}

::remotes-clone-push-fetch-pull o3 - what clone gives you::You run git clone git@github.com\:pep/shop.git on a new laptop. What do you get?{
=A local repository with the whole history and a remote named origin that points at GitHub#Correct. Clone copies every commit and sets up origin, so fetch, pull and push work right away.
~Only the latest version of the files, with no history#Not quite. A normal clone copies the whole history, not just the newest files.
~A live link where every edit you save changes GitHub immediately#Not quite. Your clone is a separate copy. Nothing reaches GitHub until you commit and push.
~An empty repository that fills in only after you run git fetch#Not quite. Clone already downloads the history and checks out the files.
}
